Title: The Late Hour
Author: Sample Page

INT. WRITER'S ROOM - NIGHT

RAVI sits at a small desk under a single lamp. A stack of blank pages in
front of him, a clock showing 2:14 AM. He writes a line, crosses it out,
writes again.

RAVI
(under his breath)
Fifty pages and the second act still won't stand up.

His phone buzzes. He ignores it, reaches for a cold cup of tea.

RAVI
Okay. What does the character actually want? Not what I want her to want.

EXT. CITY ROAD - NIGHT

RAVI walks with MEERA along an empty road. She carries a folder she never
opens.

MEERA
You keep rewriting the same scene. That's not revision, that's hiding.

RAVI
Then tell me what's wrong with it.

MEERA
Nothing's wrong with the words. You're scared the story is really about you.

RAVI stops walking.

RAVI
It is. That's the part I can't fix on paper.

CUT TO:

INT. WRITER'S ROOM - DAWN

Grey light through the window. The desk is covered in crossed-out pages,
but one page lies clean at the center. Ravi reads it aloud.

RAVI
She doesn't forgive him. She just stops carrying it.

He nods once, sets the page down gently, and picks up a fresh sheet.
